// What a Khayyam-written compiler needs second: a place to put what it has read
// so far. A compiler is a state machine over a text — a position, a symbol
// table, an output being built — and a program can only hold state in a field
// its own methods reach.
//
// The language's position: fields are always private and the only way to read or
// modify a capsule's internal state is through methods attached to that capsule
// (docs/khayyam/encapsulation.md → Capsule Structure and Privacy), and a
// receiving method cannot touch a passed capsule's fields
// (docs/khayyam/khayyam.md → Method). What a body's statement may write is not
// stated anywhere, so what the owner has settled is what a field is *for*: it is
// a variable, and a variable is handed to a method. The rule that a body may
// reach `self.field` is a rule of this toolchain rather than a claim about the
// language, and the refusal the two lines below used to meet is that rule
// working.
//
// What this probe shows: a capsule holds a position, a method of that capsule
// reaches its own field and makes a call on the value it holds, and a method of
// that capsule hands its field to the method that writes it — which is the shape
// the owner's ruling names, and which needs no more of the language than a field
// read and a variable handed over. The write itself lands in
// [run.test.ts](../test/run.test.ts), where a host supplies the method that writes
// and the test reads the field back.

tp Count cp {
}

tp Increment mt (self Count) (by Count) ()

tp Store mt (self Cursor) (value Count) (stored Count)

tp Cursor cp {
    position Count
    length Count
}

tp Advance mt (self Cursor) (step Count) (moved Count) {
    self.position.Increment(step)()
}

tp Bump mt (self Cursor) (step Count) (moved Count) {
    self.Store(step)(step)
}

tp Move mt (self Cursor) (step Count) (moved Count) {
    self.Bump(self.position, step)()
}
